# Game commentary source.

# Formatting:
# [*LINK]: Jump to a new note; also display source if possible. (Default behavior.)
# [~LINK]: Display source for LINK, but keep this note open.
# [text|*LINK], [text|~LINK]: Specify link text.
# [text|https://...] External link.
# [TITLE|relative:../GAME/#RTN:GO] Sibling game link.

ABOUT: Welcome to the commentary track!

rtn:V-VERSION: Print the title, release, and serial number. This release is branded as “interactive fiction”, replacing the “INTERLOGIC Science Fiction” label of the original release.
	(But see [glob:MAIN-MENU].)


GRAMMAR-LEGEND: This is the list of verb forms which the game recognizes. (Simple directions like `NORTH` are a special case; they’re not listed here.) These are compiled from the [SYNTAX|~src:SYNTAX-65] file.
	Objects in a verb line have various flags which give hints about their usage. An object attribute (like `LIGHTBIT`) means that if the player doesn’t name an object, the parser will try to guess one with that attribute.
	The other flag letters have specific meanings: **m**ultiple objects are accepted; the player must **h**ave the object; the player will automatically **t**ake the object; the object should be **i**n the room or **o**n the ground; the object should be **p**ossessed or **c**arried in a container. (Yes, they’re confusing. Infocom’s own docs say “No one really understands them except Stu” (Galley).)

attr:RMUNGBIT: Not applied to real objects. `RMUNGBIT` is a hack used in grammar lines; it means the object is optional (but the preposition before it is required). This allows the parser to accept commands like [LOOK AROUND|~src:SYNTAX-193].

glob:P-SYNTAX: The grammar line found when the most recent command was parsed. (Except for simple direction commands, which are a special case.) See the Grammar tab.


OBJTREE-LEGEND: In this list, `Rooms` are rooms and `Items` are regular items. (Sometimes portable, sometimes not.)
	`Scen` indicates scenery objects. These exist in multiple places; for example, the [~obj:STAIRS] are visible in many rooms.
	`Glob` indicates a global object, which is *always* visible, or at least mentionable. For example, [~obj:SLEEP] is global, so you can always type `GO TO SLEEP`.

obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS: This is a container for scenery objects like [~obj:STAIRS], which are visible from many locations in the game.

src:GLOBALS-30: This refers to the word `ZZMGCK` in [~obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS], above.
	Why is this synonym necessary? Not clear! Most Infocom games have it (or a variation), but some say “No, this synonym doesn’t need to exist” instead.
	If you type `EXAMINE ZZMGCK`, the parser finds [~obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS]. But that object is nameless, so you get a garbage reply. Go on, try it.

obj:ADVENTURER: If you refer to `ME`, you get the [obj:ME] global object, not the `ADVENTURER`.
	The `ADVENTURER` object has several irrelevant synonyms like `opener`. Since the `ADVENTURER` is never in scope, any reference to these produces a message like “You can’t see any opener here!” Presumably this was meant to avoid an “I don’t know the word” error, which would give away that there is no can opener in the game.

obj:ME: A global object which is always in scope. Commands referring to `ME` hit this, rather than [*obj:ADVENTURER], which is a stub that moves around the map.
	Because of this, parser messages mostly refer to you as “you”, even though the [obj:ADVENTURER]’s printed name is “player”.

rtn:CRETIN-F: In most earlier games, the player object was called “[cretin|relative:../zork1/#OBJ:ADVENTURER]” under the hood. Infocom has finally ditched that weak joke, but its smell lingers in the name of this routine.

rtn:V-LOOK-CRETIN: Ooh, touchy. This is the response if you type `LOOK OBJECT` instead of `LOOK AT OBJECT` or `EXAMINE OBJECT`.
	Modern IF tools like Inform 7 have generally gone back to accepting `LOOK OBJECT` as a synonym. Honestly, I’m with Meretzky on this one.

glob:WINNER: `WINNER` is the actor in the current command. 
	`WINNER` is usually [~obj:ADVENTURER], but for a command like `FLOYD, GIVE ME THE CARD` it shifts to the named NPC.

glob:HERE: The location of the [glob:WINNER].


rtn:GO: Set up the world at the start of the game. Start the timers. Print the banner. Set all those useful globals like [~glob:HERE] and [~glob:WINNER].

rtn:JIGS-UP: This routine displays your demise and then calls [rtn:FINISH].

rtn:FINISH: This routine displays your score. Then, if you haven’t reached a story conclusion, it offers a “`RESTART`, `RESTORE`, or `QUIT`” choice.
	(Infocom didn’t come up with this end-of-game convention until 1984, and they didn’t really standardize on it until 1985. This version of *Planetfall* is the first one that offers you that choice. Previous versions just asked you “In the interests of interstellar peace, would you like to play again?”)


prop:C-MOVE: This property does double duty. For rooms, it specifies extra travel time for particular directions. (That is, unusually long exit paths.)
	For the dispenser buttons on the [~obj:CHEMICAL-DISPENSER], `C-MOVE` specifies the chemical color.
	(In fact *Planetfall* only uses 26 of the 31 properties, so there’s no need to double up like this.)


glob:LIT: Whether the current location has light. Set by calling the [rtn:LIT?] routine.

rtn:LIT?: Determine whether a given location has light. This is true if the room or anything in it has [attr:ONBIT].
	This routine is used to set the [glob:LIT] variable.
	The only dark rooms in *Planetfall* are the [~obj:REACTOR-ACCESS-STAIRS] and [~obj:TRANSPORTATION-SUPPLY].

glob:LOAD-ALLOWED: The maximum weight you can carry. This decreases as you become sicker; see [rtn:I-SICKNESS-WARNINGS].
	Most Infocom games track your initial weight limit (as `LOAD-MAX`). This allows the game to restore `LOAD-ALLOWED` when you heal. But in *Planetfall*, you never return to full health! So it is sufficient to track incremental changes.

glob:LOW-DIRECTION: Properties with this value and above are directions.


attr:ONBIT: Set for glowing objects and permanently-illuminated rooms.

attr:RLANDBIT: Set for all rooms. This flag is not checked for rooms, but it has a secondary use: Floyd has `RLANDBIT` when active.

attr:ACTORBIT: Set for living beings (or friendly robots).

glob:P-WON: Set if the parser successfully parsed a command. (As opposed to a blank line or an error.)
	No relation to whether the *player* has won.

glob:C-INTS: Indicates how much of the timer table ([*glob:C-TABLE]) is used. This counts down from `C-TABLELEN`, which is 240.

glob:C-DEMONS: Indicates how much of the timer table is used for daemons.
	The difference between daemons and timers is that timers run every turn; daemons run every *input*, even if no turn passes. But *Planetfall* does not use daemons.

glob:C-TABLE: The timer table. See the “Timers” tab for a current listing.


TIMERS-LEGEND: A list of the timers running (or paused) in the timer table ([~glob:C-TABLE]). Positive numbers count down (while enabled) and call the named function after that many millichrons. Negative numbers are called every turn while the timer is enabled.
	(*Planetfall* is the first Infocom game in which timers can count down by arbitrary amounts of time. *Deadline* fakes its long `WAIT`s by calling [~rtn:CLOCKER] once per minute.)
	This list only grows as you play; timers can be added, started, and stopped, but not removed.
	
glob:C-ELAPSED: The amount of time (in millichrons) taken by the current turn. This can range from 7 up to 150 or more.
	However, the variable is [set back to 7|~src:MISC-177] at the start of each turn. So the State tab will always show it as 7, regardless of how long the last turn took. Sorry about that.

rtn:TIMELESS-VERB?: Out-of-game actions like `SCORE` and `VERBOSE` take no game time. This routine checks which verbs get this treatment.
	Interestingly, during the [escape pod ride|rtn:I-POD-TRIP] and the [shuttle ride|rtn:I-SHUTTLE], `TIMELESS-VERB?` is *not* checked. Even out-of-game verbs advance the clock during those trips.

rtn:I-RANDOM-INTERRUPTS: This timer sets up various random factors (the [explosion timer|rtn:I-BLOWUP-FEINSTEIN], the [combination lock|glob:NUMBER-NEEDED], etc) – but only *after* the player’s first move.
	Why? Almost certainly to get better random numbers. On an eight-bit home computer, the only decent source of randomness is human input. (See the [Apple II manual|https://archive.org/details/apple-ii-ref-manual/page/33/mode/1up?q=rdkey] for an example.) On such machines, the `RANDOM` opcode relies on keyboard timing to ensure that random numbers really are unpredictable.
	(In this exhibit, `RANDOM` uses Javascript’s `Math.random()` function, so the issue doesn’t arise.)

rtn:I-BLOWUP-FEINSTEIN: This is queued 240-330 millichrons after the first turn. Once it fires, it re-queues itself to run every turn for the next five turns, narrating the ship’s destruction.

rtn:I-POD-TRIP: This timer handles the pod’s trip to Resida.
	This is always twelve turns. Each of these turns takes [54 millichrons|~src:MISC-300], regardless of your actions. Even out-of-game actions like `SCORE` advance the clock during this ride.

rtn:I-SINK-POD: This timer handles the pod falling into the water and sinking. It’s triggered when you leave the [~obj:SAFETY-WEB].

rtn:I-SICKNESS-WARNINGS: The timer that handles the progression of The Disease.
	This fires every 700 millichrons, but it only has effect if [glob:SICKNESS-WARNING-FLAG] is set. That flag is set [after sleeping|~rtn:WAKING-UP] and after your [first shuttle ride|~rtn:LAWANDA-PLATFORM-F]. The effect is that you feel worse once a day (or after that shuttle ride), but with an unpredictable delay.
	
glob:SICKNESS-WARNING-FLAG: Indicates whether your [~glob:SICKNESS-LEVEL] is about to get worse. This flag is set [after sleeping|~rtn:WAKING-UP] and after your [first shuttle ride|~rtn:LAWANDA-PLATFORM-F]. It takes effect the next time [rtn:I-SICKNESS-WARNINGS] fires. (So up to 700 millichrons after sleeping, etc.)

rtn:I-SHUTTLE: This timer handles the shuttle run (in either direction).
	The run always takes 24 turns, as measured by [glob:SHUTTLE-COUNTER]. Every turn, this adjusts [glob:SHUTTLE-VELOCITY] up or down by 5 based on [~glob:LEVER-SETTING].
	The duration of a turn (during this run) is determined by [glob:SHUTTLE-VELOCITY], *not* by your actions. Even out-of-game actions like `SCORE` advance the clock during this ride.

glob:SHUTTLE-COUNTER: How far you have travelled in your current shuttle run.
	Note that the shuttle run always takes 24 game turns, regardless of velocity. See [glob:SHUTTLE-VELOCITY].

glob:SHUTTLE-VELOCITY: The units are not specified, but it increases in steps of 5. Velocity 5 is a crawl; 45 is the recommended limit. If you pass 60, a bad time is unavoidable.
	The shuttle run always takes exactly 24 *turns*, regardless of `SHUTTLE-VELOCITY`. This implies that each turn measures a fixed *distance* along the tunnel. The [glob:C-ELAPSED] time to move that distance is [600/velocity|~src:MISC-302]. That means 120 millichrons/turn at a crawl, or as low as 10/turn if you floor it.
	Infocom games almost always identify turns with time, even if abstractly. The shuttle scene is an unusual exception.

glob:SHUTTLE-RECORDING-2: *Bug:* You can activate a shuttle from the front cabin and then run to the back and drive it from there.

rtn:I-FLOYD: The general handler for Floyd following you around and doing stuff. It usually runs every turn when Floyd is active (has [attr:RLANDBIT]), but it’s given a short delay the first time you turn him on (see [here|~src:COMPONE-2074].) It’s also sometimes given a delay if Floyd wants some alone time.


rtn:KLUDGE: This is called when Floyd follows you into a new room. It handles a couple of one-time location responses.


PHONETIC: The printed language uses a phonetic spelling system. Long vowels are always doubled; “X” means “th”; “C” means “ch”; and so on. There are no silent letters.
	Amusingly, the Residan *spoken* language remains identical to Galalingua. (Floyd doesn’t seem to have an accent. Nor do [recorded announcements|~rtn:I-ANNOUNCEMENT].) This is somewhat unrealistic; real languages tend to change pronunciation faster than spelling, which is why the tough coughs as he ploughs dough.

rtn:PLAQUE-PSEUDO: See [notes on foonetik speleeng|PHONETIC].
	The Translation tab lets you see this text in regular English. I mean Galalingua.

glob:MAIN-MENU: When *Planetfall* was first released in 1983, it was billed as “INTERLOGIC Science Fiction”, in keeping with Infocom’s other games. The “Interlogic” trademark was deleted in this 1985 release. But the in-game library text was never updated to match.

glob:34-TEXT: A reference to *Suspended* as in-game history. (As distinct from the reference in “[Starkros and Suspendid|glob:63-TEXT]”, which is not exactly in-character.)
	Note that Floyd is a [B-19|~src:COMPONE-2153].

glob:51-TEXT: The “lab leak” story is a lot more fraught today than it came off as in the 1980s.

glob:62-TEXT: The entry refers to “Witnis” rather than “*Xe* Witnis”. I know I was annoyed when Infocom broke its pattern of one-word titles. Perhaps Meretzky agreed.

glob:63-TEXT: But see also “[Roobotiks|glob:34-TEXT]” and the [Ballad of the Starcrossed Miner|src:COMPTWO-2088].


glob:DIAL-NUMBER: The current setting of the [~obj:COMBINATION-DIAL].

glob:NUMBER-NEEDED: The correct combination for the [~obj:COMBINATION-DIAL]. The value is set *after* your first move; see [rtn:I-RANDOM-INTERRUPTS].
	Note that peeking at this value in the State tab won’t help you much! The [~obj:TELEPORTATION-CARD] is in the same place as the [~obj:COMBINATION-PAPER] clue. This must have been a guard against brute-force guessing; it serves equally well against Visible Zorker cheats.


glob:DREAMS: The dream sequences are a mix of silliness, foreboding, and in-jokes. Just like real life, really.

src:GLOBALS-2182: This dream can occur (with a low probability) after you’ve reached the Lawanda Complex.
	(This is a slight logic hole, since you can retrieve both the [~obj:LOWER-ELEVATOR-CARD] and the [~obj:SHUTTLE-CARD] without activating Floyd and thus learning his name.)
	
src:GLOBALS-2215: This dream is a reference to *Zork 1*. It also gives an understated hint for a puzzle later in *Planetfall*.

src:GLOBALS-2222: This dream may be a reference to *Starcross*. It’s also the only diegetic title-drop in *Planetfall*.
	(No, I’m not counting the [~obj:BROCHURE].)


src:COMPTWO-2088: This is of course a summary of *Starcross*. That game took place in the year 2186. “Ten thousand years before” gives some sense of the time scale of the Third Galactic Union. (See also [rtn:GRUE-F].)
	The poetry is bunk. Still, as a kid, I was impressed by the idea of one adventurer’s history gradually turning into another adventurer’s ancient myth.

rtn:GRUE-F: The “visiting alien spacecraft” is another historic reference to *Starcross*. See also the [Ballad of the Starcrossed Miner|src:COMPTWO-2088].

obj:GRUE: Hello, grue!

obj:TROLL: You might not recognize the humanoid as the troll from Zork 1, but the source listing makes it clear.

obj:RAT-ANT: Related to the rat-ants from *Starcross*? Heck of a lot bigger, though.

obj:TRIFFID: A [classical monster|https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triffid] rather than an Infocom reference.


src:VERBS-1649: This compiled-out routine would have been invoked with the command `Z`. (In the compiled game, `Z` is just a synonym for `WAIT`.) 
	The symbol `QUITTER` is the MDL interrupt handler. (See [MDL docs|https://mdl-language.readthedocs.io/en/latest/21-interrupts/].) On Infocom’s mainframe interpreter, this routine sent it a `ctrl-G`, which would have paused the interpreter and allowed low-level debugging.

src:VERBS-1894: A number of handy cheat codes, now commented out. The commands would have been `$CRAG`, `$FORK`, `$CARDS`, `$BOOTH`, `$FIX`.

src:VERBS-1918: This is a quote from _Alice’s Restaurant_, not a harsh indictment of the cheater. Or, well, both, I suppose.

rtn:PRINT-OUT-F: The `FIXED-FONT` declarations be wrapped only around the tabular status report, not this entire message.

src:COMPTWO-1530: I wonder what this was attached to? Note that the message is a whimsical mashup of the endings of *Zork 1*, *Zork 3*, *Deadline*, and *Starcross*.

src:COMPONE-2228: This was a somewhat obscure reference even in the 1980s. The first human-built nuclear reactor was an experiment in a disused [squash court|https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago_Pile-1] beneath the University of Chicago.

const:SERIAL: This is not the public serial number (“851003”), but an internal serial or build number. Note that while it’s defined as 0 here, the compiled game file has the value 1. `SERIAL` must be updated by the compiler during the build process.
	To see this value, type the debug command `$VERIFY 502`.

src:VERBS-1575: If you type the debug command `$VERIFY 502`, the game displays the [const:SERIAL] value instead of doing the normal verification.

src:COMPONE-1670: A commented-out digging machine. A [part|~src:COMPTWO-1739] for it (also commented out) would have been found in the [~obj:LAB-UNIFORM], so this was probably a discarded puzzle idea.

src:COMPTWO-1739: A commented-out part for the [digging machine|src:COMPONE-1670] (also commented out) in the [~obj:MACHINE-SHOP].

src:COMPTWO-3049: This last message is not compiled into the game file. It’s effectively an `ELSE` for the “[~glob:WARMTH-FLAG] > 10” `COND`, which already has an `ELSE` clause directly above (the “greedily devours” response). So it can never run, and the compiler optimizes it out.
	This is obvious because the Visible Planetfall has reformatted the routine to a consistent indendation! In the original source file, this clause is lined up with the *outer* `COND`. It’s clearly meant to apply to all objects other than the hot laser, but it’s at the wrong nesting level. The original indentation disguised this mistake.



# The two islands are 20 km (12 miles) apart. The shuttle ride takes 24 turns at 600/v millichrons per turn. We may consistently assume that each turn covers half a mile.
# So the recommended SHUTTLE-VELOCITY of "45" covers 12 miles in 320 millichrons, or 46 minutes.
# This puts the shuttle speed limit at a blazing 16 mph! 
# The "recommended" run (capped at 45) takes 763 millichrons, or 110 minutes.
# If you leave the lever pushed, you can hit 20 mph by the time you reach turnover at the midpoint. That only shaves 14 millichrons (two minutes) off the trip time.

# But then an elevator ride takes 100mc / 15 minutes.
